---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title = "About LLMSX";
const description = "Who maintains LLMSX, who it serves, what the guides and tools offer, and how to report a correction or get in touch.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} route="/about/">
  <p class="lede">Practical AI workflows, with the work behind them available to inspect.</p>
  <p>LLMSX, also known as LLMS-Explorer, is an independent open-source project maintained by <a href="https://github.com/mithudso">Mitchell Hudson</a>. The site serves developers and technical users who want to keep useful context, investigate a technical question and use agents with more explicit evidence.</p>
  <h2>What you will find here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/blog/">Guides and research</a>: experiments, implementation reports, troubleshooting records and architecture discussions. Each kind of article supports a different kind of conclusion.</li>
    <li><a href="/downloads/">Open tools</a>: LLMSX Explorer for research, Skills Explorer for installed skills, and GitHub Explorer for repository work. Each has its own requirements and installation instructions.</li>
    <li><a href="/skills/">Agent skills</a>: reusable instructions you can read before adding them to a supported harness.</li>
    <li><a href="/tree/">A reference library</a>: topic pages, source-linked facts and downloadable context. It includes agent-assisted research and mirrored reports.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What makes the work useful</h2>
  <p>A good guide leaves you with a next step you can perform and a result you can check. An experiment adds its setup, raw evidence and limits. The <a href="/blog/what-docs-llms-files-and-indexes-actually-save/">256-run documentation experiment</a>, for example, reports where llms files and indexes did not help as well as where documentation did.</p>
  <p>The library is broader than the site's authored guides. A source-linked fact is a starting point for investigation, not a promise that a model will answer correctly. Read the <a href="/editorial/">source and editorial standards</a> to understand that distinction.</p>
  <h2 id="contact">Contact and corrections</h2>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:mitchphudson@gmail.com">mitchphudson@gmail.com</a> for questions about the project or private account and privacy requests. For a public correction, <a href="https://github.com/mithudso/llms-explorer/issues">open a repository issue</a> with the page URL, the disputed claim and the source or reproduction that supports the correction.</p>
  <p>Keep credentials and private account details out of public issues. Tool bugs belong with the tool's linked source repository so the report reaches the right code.</p>
  <h2>Source and reuse</h2>
  <p>The <a href="https://github.com/mithudso/llms-explorer">repository</a> contains the website, tools and published research artifacts. Check the licence attached to a tool or source before reusing it; mirrored material retains its own provenance. <a href="/start-here/">Start with a small workflow</a> if you are new to the site.</p>
</Base>
